  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-BGD230</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Effective gender responsive and inclusive social dialogue and dispute resolution mechanisms established</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bangladesh&#x2019;s labour relations system is marred by ineffective social dialogue, collective bargaining, and dispute resolution. Mechanisms for tripartism and social dialogue remain weak and fragmented, with limited sectoral platforms beyond the Ready-Made Garment (RMG) sector. Wage-setting processes lack consistency, with infrequent revisions and gender pay gaps, while national minimum wage remains outdated. The labour dispute resolution system faces capacity constraints, backlogs, and unclear mandates, leading to industrial tensions that undermine harmonious workplace relations.
ILO&#x2019;s strategy focuses on partners&#x2019; capacities and dialogue institutions and processes in line with international labour standards, and improved dispute resolution policies, alternative dispute mechanisms, case management systems, and capacities for conciliators and tripartite constituents. ILO works to enhance dialogue structures at national, sectoral, and enterprise levels; expand sectoral tripartite mechanisms beyond RMG; and strengthen the Tripartite Consultative Council. ILO will work on gender-responsive National Wage Policy, wage-setting systems, and addressing wage gaps. In the RMG sector and beyond, ILO continues advising on social dialogue, compliance, gender equality, Human Rights and Due Diligence (HRDD), and environmental sustainability, aiming to scale good practices.
Expected results include effective labour dispute resolution systems, strengthened social dialogue mechanisms at all levels, and enhanced institutional capacity to promote fair wages and decent work time. Beneficiaries are workers, employers, trade unions, and labour administration bodies, for equitable, inclusive, and sustainable industrial relations.
Key partners include the Ministry of Labour and Employment, Department of Labour, Department of Inspection for Factories and Establishments, employers&#x2019; and workers&#x2019; organizations, chambers of commerce, and international development partners.</narrative>
  </description>
